        <mxCell id="o_sec4" value="D — KEY TRADE-OFFS (each choice vs its named alternative)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="2960" width="800" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="o_to" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Point stream in Cassandra (7d TTL) + polyline in S3&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;vs&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;everything in Postgres&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;everything in S3&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;a TSDB&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;Postgres can't take 100k–500k append/s and the rows are write-once, never updated — no need for its transactional machinery. Pure S3 can't take small&lt;br&gt;in-flight appends. A TSDB is built for aggregation over metrics, not for retrieving one entity's ordered stream. Cassandra's (partition, clustering) model is&lt;br&gt;exactly &quot;append ordered points to one activity, read them all back once.&quot; &lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; no joins, no transactions, and a two-store consistency problem —&lt;br&gt;the polyline in S3 and the stats in Postgres can diverge if the processor dies mid-write, so processing is idempotent and re-runnable from Cassandra.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Storage math that forces this:&lt;/b&gt; 200M activities/day x 100 KB = &lt;b&gt;20 TB/day ≈ 7 PB/yr&lt;/b&gt;. Raw points at ~30 B x 360/activity ≈ 11 KB, so a delta-encoded&lt;br&gt;polyline compresses ~5–10x → ~2–3 TB/day in S3. That difference is the whole reason the canonical copy is a blob and Cassandra is a 7-day buffer.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Async matching off Kafka&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;vs&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;matching inline in the complete request&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;Inline couples a p99 mobile response to a multi-second CPU-bound geometry job at 2k/s. Async gives retries, DLQ, independent scaling, and replay when the&lt;br&gt;segment table changes. &lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; efforts aren't visible the instant the activity ends — which is exactly why the 3-min leaderboard staleness NFR exists.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;S2/geohash cell index for candidate segments&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;vs&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;PostGIS ST_Intersects per activity&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;brute-force compare against all segments&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;Brute force is O(activities x segments) — millions of comparisons per activity, hopeless. PostGIS works but puts 2k/s of heavy geometry queries on the&lt;br&gt;transactional DB. An in-memory cell→segment map is a hash lookup per cell and the whole index (few million segments) fits in RAM per worker.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; index must be rebuilt/broadcast when segments change, and cell granularity is a tuning knob (too coarse = many candidates, too fine = huge index).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Redis ZSET per segment&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;vs&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;SQL ORDER BY elapsedSec LIMIT 50&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;ZSET gives O(log N) rank-of-a-member — the &quot;you are #412&quot; number is the expensive part in SQL, which needs a window function over the whole segment.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; memory cost, and it's a derived cache — Postgres SegmentEffort stays the source of truth so the ZSET can be rebuilt after a Redis loss.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Idempotency note:&lt;/b&gt; use ZADD with the absolute elapsedSec, never ZINCRBY — a redelivered Kafka message must be a no-op, not a double-count.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hybrid feed fan-out&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;vs&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;pure fan-out-on-write&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;pure fan-out-on-read&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;Fan-out-on-write hits 200ms p99 trivially but a pro athlete with 500k followers turns one activity into 500k writes. Fan-out-on-read is cheap to write but&lt;br&gt;makes every feed load a scatter-gather across hundreds of followees. Hybrid: precompute for normal users, pull-and-merge at read time for the few&lt;br&gt;thousand celebrity accounts. &lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; two code paths and a merge step, plus a threshold that needs tuning.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="3000" width="2340" height="620" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="o_sec5" value="E — DEEP DIVES" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="3670" width="600" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="o_dd1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;1. THE CORE HARD PROBLEM — segment matching&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Naive:&lt;/b&gt; for each finished activity, compare its route against every segment's polyline.&lt;br&gt;With millions of segments x 2k activities/s this is astronomically wrong.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix — candidate generation then verification:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Cover each segment's polyline with S2 cells (~level 13, roughly 1 km) at&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;segment-creation time; build cellId → [segmentId] and hold it in worker memory.&lt;br&gt;2. For a finished activity, compute the set of cells its route touches (a few hundred).&lt;br&gt;3. Union the segments in those cells → typically tens to low hundreds of candidates.&lt;br&gt;4. &lt;b&gt;Verify each candidate:&lt;/b&gt; find the route point nearest the segment start (within&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;~15 m), then walk the route forward checking it stays within a corridor of the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;segment polyline &lt;i&gt;in order&lt;/i&gt;, until the segment end. Frechet-style corridor check,&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;not point-set overlap — otherwise running the road backwards would match.&lt;br&gt;5. &lt;b&gt;elapsedSec&lt;/b&gt; = interpolated timestamp at exit − at entry. Interpolate, don't snap:&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;a 10 s sample gap at 30 km/h is 83 m of positional error on a 400 m segment.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Edge cases a senior raises:&lt;/b&gt; multiple laps of the same segment in one activity (emit&lt;br&gt;the best effort, keep all); partial traversal (reject); a route that joins mid-segment&lt;br&gt;(reject); GPS drift in urban canyons (accuracy field gates the match).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#F8CECC;strokeColor=#B85450;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="3710" width="760" height="430" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="o_dd2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;2. THE SCALE-BREAK — push it 10–50x&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;First break: the traffic model itself.&lt;/b&gt; 200M activities/day at ~40 min each ⇒&lt;br&gt;200M x 2400 s / 86400 s ≈ &lt;b&gt;5.5M concurrent recorders&lt;/b&gt;, not 1M. So ingest is&lt;br&gt;~550k points/s, 5x the stated figure. Batching every 60 s brings the &lt;i&gt;request&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;rate to ~90k req/s and the &lt;i&gt;row&lt;/i&gt; rate stays 550k/s — fine for Cassandra spread&lt;br&gt;across activityId partitions, which have no hot key by construction.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Second break: the matcher is CPU-bound.&lt;/b&gt; At 2k finished activities/s, if&lt;br&gt;verification costs ~50 ms of CPU per activity that's 100 cores steady-state,&lt;br&gt;before peak. Peak is not uniform — Saturday 9am in Europe is many times the&lt;br&gt;mean. Fixes: partition Kafka by activityId and scale consumers horizontally;&lt;br&gt;accept lag at peak and let the 3-min staleness SLA absorb it; shed nothing.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Third break: 10x growth ⇒ 70 PB/yr.&lt;/b&gt; Tiering becomes mandatory — recent&lt;br&gt;polylines on standard storage, older ones to Glacier, and stats stay hot in&lt;br&gt;Postgres so the profile page never touches cold storage.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fourth break: a mass-participation event.&lt;/b&gt; 50k runners on the same course&lt;br&gt;within 3 hours ⇒ one segment's ZSET takes a huge write burst and everyone&lt;br&gt;reads that same leaderboard. See hot keys →&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#F8CECC;strokeColor=#B85450;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="3710" width="760" height="430" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="o_dd3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;3. HOT KEYS &amp;amp; HOT PARTITIONS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Viral segment&lt;/b&gt; (Alpe d'Huez, a marathon course): one ZSET key takes&lt;br&gt;concentrated reads and writes. Mitigate with a read-through local cache in the&lt;br&gt;Leaderboard Service (top-100 is what 99% of viewers ask for, TTL 60 s — well&lt;br&gt;inside the 3-min budget), and Redis replicas serving reads. Deep-page requests&lt;br&gt;fall through to Postgres.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Celebrity author:&lt;/b&gt; handled by the hybrid fan-out, not by the datastore.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;No hot partition on ingest:&lt;/b&gt; activityId is effectively random — this is why it&lt;br&gt;is the partition key rather than userId.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;4. CONSISTENCY MODEL&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Activity metadata: read-your-writes for the author (route the author's read to&lt;br&gt;the primary), eventual for everyone else.&lt;br&gt;Feed and leaderboard: eventual, bounded — the &lt;code&gt;asOf&lt;/code&gt; field in the leaderboard&lt;br&gt;response makes the bound explicit rather than a silent lie.&lt;br&gt;Postgres SegmentEffort is the source of truth; the ZSET is a rebuildable cache.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tie-breaking:&lt;/b&gt; equal elapsedSec ⇒ earlier achievedAt ranks higher. Deterministic&lt;br&gt;ordering matters or pagination cursors skip and duplicate rows.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#F8CECC;strokeColor=#B85450;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1620" y="3710" width="740" height="430" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="o_dd4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;5. OPERABILITY — how do you know it's stale or wrong?&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;i&gt;(the L5-vs-L4 section, and the one most often skipped)&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Freshness as a first-class metric:&lt;/b&gt; emit &lt;code&gt;activityCompletedAt → effortVisibleAt&lt;/code&gt; as a histogram and alert on p99 &amp;gt; 3 min — the NFR becomes a monitor, not a sentence in a doc.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Kafka consumer lag&lt;/b&gt; per consumer group, alerted before it eats the staleness budget. Lag spikes at deploy time from partition rebalancing are the classic cause.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Correctness monitors:&lt;/b&gt; matched-efforts-per-activity distribution (a sudden drop means the segment index failed to load); DLQ depth; % of activities where the polyline exists in S3&lt;br&gt;but stats are missing in Postgres (the two-store divergence).&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Anti-cheat / data quality:&lt;/b&gt; reject or flag efforts implying impossible speed for the activity type (a 60 km/h &quot;run&quot;), GPS teleports between consecutive points, and accuracy&lt;br&gt;values above threshold. Without this the all-time leaderboard is permanently topped by a car.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Backfill / replay:&lt;/b&gt; when a segment is added or edited, replay recent activities from Kafka (or the S3 polylines) through the matcher. This is why the polyline is durable and&lt;br&gt;processing is idempotent.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Failure behaviour:&lt;/b&gt; ingest must stay up at 99.99% — it is the only path that can lose unrecoverable user data. If Cassandra is degraded, buffer to Kafka and drain later;&lt;br&gt;the client's on-disk buffer plus idempotent upserts means a full ingest outage degrades to delayed uploads, not lost activities. Feed and leaderboard may serve stale or&lt;br&gt;fail soft — a blank leaderboard is an inconvenience, a lost 5-hour ride is a churned user.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cost at target scale:&lt;/b&gt; S3 dominates (~2–3 TB/day of polylines ≈ 1 PB/yr, growing forever) — lifecycle policies and the raw-vs-compressed decision are a real budget line,&lt;br&gt;not a footnote. Matcher CPU is second. Redis memory for leaderboards is comparatively trivial.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#D5E8D4;strokeColor=#82B366;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="4180" width="2340" height="300" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
